      "role": "user",
      "content": "wait, what? i'm just trying to fix my cabinets. why are you talking about goblins and swords? i need actual technical advice on how to stop the splintering, not metaphors. can we just stick to the wood grain and tool sharpness?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy, didn't I? Let's get back to the technical aspects of dovetailing. Yes, wood grain and tool sharpness are crucial factors to consider.\n\nIn terms of wood grain, it's possible that the walnut is growing in a direction that's causing the pins to split. You might want to try cutting the pins at a slightly different angle, following the direction of the wood grain, rather than directly across it. This can help reduce the likelihood of splintering.\n\nAs for tool sharpness, a dull blade can indeed cause the wood to tear out. Make sure your blade is sharp and well-maintained. If you're using a hand saw, try sharpening it regularly. If you're using a power tool, such as a table saw or a router, ensure that the blade is properly aligned and adjusted.\n\nAdditionally, you might want to consider using a dovetail jig or a specialized dovetail cutter, which can help guide the blade and reduce the risk of splintering. These tools can be a bit more expensive, but they can also help you achieve more precise and consistent cuts.\n\nI hope these suggestions are more helpful, and I apologize again for the goblin",
